virglrenderer: Venus refuses a structure type twice in a chain

What: vkr refuses a guest call whose chain has one structure type twice (Vulkan forbids it:
VUID-<struct>-sType-unique) in every chain a vkr check reads: image create info (also the
vkGetDeviceImage* queries), image binds, image memory requirements, sampler, buffer and
buffer view create info, descriptor set layout, descriptor writes (also push descriptors),
set allocation and pool create info, render pass create info (v1, v2 and each v2 subpass),
render pass begin, framebuffer, a secondary's inheritance, dynamic rendering, shader stages,
graphics pipelines (with their vertex input, multisample and color blend states) and compute
pipelines. Each goes through its call's refusal path (a create the guest waits for gets its
error, the rest end the app's Venus context). vkr_chain_repeat compares each struct with
the ones before it and stops at the first repeat. #664 did the same for submits, sparse
binds and memory allocation (vkr_chain_repeats).
Why: vkr's checks read the first struct of a type (vkr_find_struct) and the Mac's drivers
the last. Mesa's vk_common_CreateRenderPass (KosmicKrisp) keeps the last
VkRenderPassMultiviewCreateInfo: masks [0x1, 0x1ff] passed vkr's check and the pass's begin
ended QEMU in Metal ("maxVertexAmplificationCount (9) is not supported"), and Mesa reads
that last struct's masks for every subpass, past its own array when it has fewer.
MoltenVK's vkCmdBeginRenderPass keeps the last VkRenderPassAttachmentBeginInfo: vkr checked
and held the first one's views, so MoltenVK could read past the last one's array or use a
view the app destroyed. MoltenVK and Mesa keep the last of others too (plane info, stencil
usage, vertex divisors, ycbcr, border and reduction info).
Left alone: VkDeviceCreateInfo (repeats of VkDevicePrivateDataCreateInfo are legal; vkr
reads robustness2 nullDescriptor, which MoltenVK refuses in any struct and Mesa ORs, so the
driver has at least what vkr read; maintenance6, which MoltenVK's NULL index buffer path
does not need; the texel buffer alignment, which no offset ends the process on), the image
format query (vkr changes its first external info: a repeat asks the driver about DMA_BUF,
which is unsupported, no crash), views (vkr reads nothing in their chains), output chains
and vkr's own ring and queue chains. Exceptions the VUIDs allow (Metal export and import
structs) are not in venus-protocol: the decoder ends the context on them before vkr. The
guest's Venus driver keeps one slot per type when it rewrites a chain, so legal input never
reaches vkr with a repeat.
Upstream: OmacVM policy (ADR 0065).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-commands.c (the r-repeat- cases, also under libgmalloc) and
Tests/virgl/test-venus-image-limits.c (the repeat- cases).

